WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.981 --> 00:00:13.560
My name is A-Trak, I'm a DJ, producer,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.364
record label owner.

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:21.819
The sort of, like, science of DJing,

00:00:21.819 --> 00:00:25.079
if you try to boil it
down too much to a simple

00:00:25.079 --> 00:00:28.169
description of beatmatching, for example,

00:00:28.169 --> 00:00:30.849
definitely like an intangible to it.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:34.068
On a very simple level,
beatmatching is what you

00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:38.169
call matching the tempo of
one song to the next song

00:00:38.169 --> 00:00:41.089
that's playing so that you
have a continuous beat.

00:00:41.089 --> 00:00:43.929
And just going back to
the basic idea that DJs

00:00:43.929 --> 00:00:45.448
keep people dancing.

00:00:45.448 --> 00:00:47.569
You're able to keep the
tempo steady as you go from

00:00:47.569 --> 00:00:49.079
song to song.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.959
It's actually a pretty easy
technique, but DJing itself

00:00:58.959 --> 00:01:03.704
is not that easy and I think
the real role of the DJ is

00:01:03.704 --> 00:01:06.803
selection, it's picking
songs and having a sense of

00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:08.853
what to play and when.

00:01:08.853 --> 00:01:12.523
And that's the part that's
harder to break down or explain.

00:01:12.523 --> 00:01:14.668
What really differentiates a good DJ

00:01:14.668 --> 00:01:16.668
from a not so good DJ is

00:01:18.118 --> 00:01:20.406
selection and taste and sense of timing

00:01:20.406 --> 00:01:22.948
and also knowing how to
adapt to different crowds.

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:25.896
That's probably the biggest
thing that DJs have to learn

00:01:25.896 --> 00:01:29.558
is to understand not only
what to play and when

00:01:29.558 --> 00:01:31.006
but in which setting.

00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:33.165
You can't play the same set everywhere.

00:01:33.165 --> 00:01:36.046
Electronic music's been
around since the late '70s,

00:01:36.046 --> 00:01:37.124
early '80s.

00:01:37.124 --> 00:01:41.118
So electronic music isn't
new but EDM is this newer,

00:01:41.118 --> 00:01:45.006
bigger, more polished manifestation of it.

00:01:45.006 --> 00:01:47.958
And when it really broke in
America, at the beginning of

00:01:47.958 --> 00:01:51.438
this decade, it's also
when certain songs were

00:01:51.438 --> 00:01:55.238
really bona fide pop songs,
and were playing on the radio.

00:01:55.238 --> 00:01:58.777
New audiences embraced
these big records that had a

00:01:58.777 --> 00:02:02.246
universal appeal, and by
the same token embraced

00:02:02.246 --> 00:02:05.166
the producers and DJs who
were behind those records.

00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:08.297
More and more of these
festivals started popping up.

00:02:08.297 --> 00:02:10.537
I think that confused a lot of people.

00:02:10.537 --> 00:02:13.766
Just seeing a DJ essentially play songs

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.683
that were already made,

00:02:16.788 --> 00:02:19.925
on the stage where
they'd be used to seeing

00:02:19.925 --> 00:02:21.846
the Red Hot Chili Peppers or something.

00:02:21.846 --> 00:02:24.084
People were questioning whether

00:02:24.084 --> 00:02:26.103
DJs were really just pressing

00:02:26.103 --> 00:02:28.804
play and that became a bit of a buzzword,

00:02:28.804 --> 00:02:30.976
"pressing play" or "button pusher."

00:02:30.976 --> 00:02:34.753
The range of different
styles that started existing

00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:37.963
got almost too big to just
categorize as one thing.

00:02:37.963 --> 00:02:41.835
So, do some DJs just go
up and press play and

00:02:41.835 --> 00:02:44.765
phone in the same set at a lot of events?

00:02:44.765 --> 00:02:46.016
Yeah.

00:02:46.016 --> 00:02:47.947
Does that mean that all DJs do that, no.

00:02:47.947 --> 00:02:51.315
A lot of DJs have true
craftsmanship and art

00:02:51.315 --> 00:02:52.576
to what they do.

00:02:52.576 --> 00:02:54.684
Usually the argument that
most people will give

00:02:54.684 --> 00:02:56.696
and that I was just giving
also is that DJs don't

00:02:56.696 --> 00:02:58.136
really play the same
set everywhere and we're

00:02:58.136 --> 00:02:59.765
mixing live and all this stuff.

00:02:59.765 --> 00:03:03.555
But in some cases when the
show is so big, that you

00:03:03.555 --> 00:03:06.395
get into a place where there
actually is literally pyro

00:03:06.395 --> 00:03:10.474
and effects that need to
be timed and this and that,

00:03:10.474 --> 00:03:13.294
then you'll have a DJ who plans their set.

00:03:13.294 --> 00:03:15.915
But even that to me, can
be compared to theater.

00:03:15.915 --> 00:03:20.285
When you go watch theater,
it's pre-planned but it's

00:03:20.285 --> 00:03:21.711
great entertainment.

00:03:21.711 --> 00:03:24.008
In the case of a DJ, the
DJ is doing a sort of

00:03:24.008 --> 00:03:27.188
mise-en-scène, of songs
that they know the crowd

00:03:27.188 --> 00:03:28.508
will enjoy hearing.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:31.479
And songs that they are
sometimes connected to or

00:03:31.479 --> 00:03:32.620
that they made.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.026
And translating that into
a show that will work

00:03:36.026 --> 00:03:37.448
in front of 60,000 people.

00:03:37.448 --> 00:03:39.698
There's an art to that too.

00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:45.719
The control of energy is kind of magical

00:03:45.719 --> 00:03:46.917
and undeniable.

00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:48.820
And that's a big part of the art too.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.087
So between that and

00:03:51.087 --> 00:03:53.546
the sort of sonic wizardry

00:03:53.546 --> 00:03:56.317
that happens with the knobs on here,

00:03:56.317 --> 00:04:00.220
it definitely isn't
something to be diminished.

